00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:25.300
This first video will be a short history of
Godzilla movies.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.720
I know some of you commented that you’ve
seen them all, but Godzilla and Kaiju films

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.590
in general have this really cool history that
goes all the way back to World War II and

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.070
the use of Nuclear Bombs.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.090
So I’m going to do a quick history of the
franchise and then rank my top 10 movies.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.219
There’s really 32 core Godzilla movies if
you count the American ones.

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:46.600
If you’re a purist, then you probably only
count the 28 that have been Japanese Productions

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.600
but I’m just going to include everything.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.710
There was even a separate ripoff Godzilla
movie Kim Jong IL made in North Korea in 1985.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:57.219
He wanted his own Godzilla movie so bad they
kidnapped a South Korean Director and forced

00:00:57.219 --> 00:00:58.430
him to make it.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.430
Yikes.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:05.210
The franchise itself is broken into three
periods that denote the different styles that

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.210
informed each era.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.580
They are Showa era, the Hesei era and the
Millenium Era.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.750
Those are named after the Japanese Emperors
who reigned when the films were made.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.200
Although the current Japanese Emperor crosses
both those last two.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.630
The first Godzilla movie was released in 1954
and became a cultural metaphor for the nuclear

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.890
holocaust in Japan during World War II.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.500
Like a way to deal with the horrible reality
of what happened to the Japanese people.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.020
But as the films evolved through that first
era of the franchise the movies started to

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.759
get really light-hearted and playful.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:38.780
The Godzilla monster even turned into a superhero
and they started making Godzilla versus movies.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.490
Then in 1984 Japanese directors rebooted the
series with a new storyline.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:49.410
That era lasted until 1995 and used Godzilla
more as a metaphor for Genetic testing.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:54.620
It dealt a lot more with the biology of the
monster.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:59.120
Then the current era changed the continuity
again.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.969
All the Japanese movie basically use that
first Godzilla movie as a jumping off point

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:04.030
and just go in all different directions.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.220
So each one isn’t a sequel to the ones that
came before.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:11.030
The American reboot from Roland Emmerich in
1998 is also a totally separate timeline as

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.360
is the case with the 2014 movie that’s happening
right now.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.750
So the movie we’re all going to see next
week sets up a whole new backstory and any

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.650
sequels will just flow from that.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.620
So here’s my Top 10 Godzilla Movies, not
including the current one, which I haven’t

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.620
seen yet.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.160
Number 10 - Godzilla Tokyo SOS
This is the 2003 film.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.730
It’s basically a battle royale involving
Mechagodzilla which is actually named Kiryu

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.200
which is more like a cyborg and Mothra.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.870
Number 9 - Godzilla VS Space Godzilla

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:45.370
Evil doppelganger Godzilla created from black
hole radiation and genetic material from Godzilla

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.150
carried into space by Mothra.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.489
I always love creative use of Godzilla biology.

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:54.810
Number 8 - Son of Godzilla

00:02:54.810 --> 00:03:01.250
This jump starts the idea of Godzilla reproduction
which the new movie pays homage to in a really

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:02.849
interesting way.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:06.560
Number 7 - Giant Monsters All Out Attack

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.130
Revived Godzilla possessed by souls of fallen
japanese World War II soldiers.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:16.989
There’s this really awesome story device
that deals with the concept of the Guardian

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:18.720
Monsters too.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.500
Number 6 - Return of Godzilla 1985 Reboot

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:27.480
This was produced as part of the 30 year anniversary
celebration of the original Godzilla movie

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.990
and did what most reboots do.

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:33.730
Dark Knighted the franchise making it way
darker way grittier.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.310
Number 5 - Terror Of Mechagodzilla

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:42.680
This was the last film in the original era
before the 1985 reboot.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.250
Number 4 - Mothra VS Godzilla

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.620
This was the first film Mothra appeared in.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:51.790
It’s generally regarded from a story and
production standpoint to be one of the best

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:55.250
old Godzilla movies and best from the franchise
in general next to the first film.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:04:00.830
So if you want to go way back, this is a good
place to start.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.519
Number 3 - Godzilla 2000

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:10.019
This was the last movie that was part of the
core godzilla run that got released in US

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:13.000
theaters before the new movie.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.220
It’s a little weird in that it was released
so close to the Roland Emmerich 1998 reboot,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:24.620
which was just awful.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.990
Number 2 - Gojira

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.840
This is the original 1954 film and if you
want to watch it, Criterion did a special

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.000
DVD release that’s just amazing.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.550
So if you really want to do it right.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.190
Check out the Criterion edition.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:41.550
and my Number 1 Godzilla movie - Godzilla
Vs Destoroyah

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:53.370
I made this my number one mostly because it’s
one of the best, but it’s a lot like the

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.150
Death of Superman in that it promised to kill
Godzilla for real.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:58.150
To make way for the new reboot they were going
to kill off the original godzilla.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:02.680
There was all this marketing and culturally
there was just something going on in the mid

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.830
90s where superheros were dying like it was
their job.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:11.110
DC killed Superman in 1992, they broke Batman’s
back in 1993.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:16.700
Cut to Godzilla dying in 1995 to make way
for a franchise reboot comic book style.

